{"type":"assistant","message":{"type":"message","model":"kimi-k3","usage":{"input_tokens":36314,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_5_m_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_1_h_tokens":0},"role":"assistant","id":"04026ae7a5e10a7a9fc8fb36f6031d8a","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Workspace is empty (8:2). Good.\n\nI have the full brief. Now I need to build 11 frames in 03 · Workspace. Let me check the skill resource first — the use_figma tool description says to load figma-use guidance via skill://figma/figma-use/SKILL.md MCP resource. I don't have a direct resource reading tool... Actually I have only the four tools. There's no read-resource tool available. The instruction says \"prefer the /figma-use skill if available, otherwise read the skill://figma/figma-use/SKILL.md MCP resource if available\". I don't have tools to read MCP resources listed. I'll proceed with my knowledge of the Figma Plugin API, being careful with the gotchas mentioned:\n- Inter font styles — not needed, we use Volkhov and Poppins.\n- Use `await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page)` to change pages.\n- Never use loadAllPagesAsync, setPluginData, createImageAsync.\n\nKey approach: The media components live on page 1:4. I can create instances of those components in my workspace. To instantiate a component from another page, I can use `figma.getNodeByIdAsync('6:32')` and if it's a component, call `createInstance()`. But the workspace is on another page. Instances must be created on the current page? Actually `component.createInstance()` creates an instance as a child of the component's parent page... no — createInstance() adds the instance to the same page as the component? Let me recall: `createInstance()` creates an instance \"with the same properties\" and parents it to... Actually, per the Figma API, `component.createInstance()` creates an instance of the component and it's added as a child of `figma.currentPage`. Hmm, actually I believe createInstance() adds to the current page. The docs say: \"Creates an instance of this component, with the same properties and children as the original component.\" It's added to `figma.currentPage`? I recall it gets added to the same page as the component node. To be safe, I'll set current page to the workspace page first, then createInstance() — but if it lands on the media page, I can reparent with `workspacePage.appendChild(instance)` or append into a frame directly. Reparenting across pages is allowed via appendChild.\n\nPlan of execution:\n\n1. Set current page to Workspace (8:2) via setCurrentPageAsync.\n2. Build design tokens: colors, text styles (maybe skip text styles; just use direct fonts).\n3. Create 11 frames with exact names:\n   - 01_Home (1920 wide, tall)\n   - 02_Shop\n   - 03_Product\n   - 04_Mini_Cart (product page behind + drawer)\n   - 05_Cart\n   - 06_Checkout\n   - 07_Sign_Up (1920×1080)\n   - 08_Sign_In (1920×1080)\n   - 09_Forgot_Password (1920×1080)\n   - 10_Confirmation_Code (1920×1080)\n   - 11_New_Password (1920×1080)\n4. Add prototype interactions via reactions.\n\nGiven the tool code limit of 50000 chars per call and complexity, I'll build in sections with multiple use_figma calls:\n- Call A: Setup — fonts load, helper functions, tokens, components (button, text field recreations? The brief says reuse components from 02 · Components — \"Reuse the components in 02 · Components\". I can instantiate the Button (1:24) and Text Field (1:27) components in my screens. Good idea: use those instances where buttons/fields appear. But overriding their text may be needed (e.g., button label \"Buy Now\"). Instance text override works via finding text node inside instance and setting characters. That works.\n\nBut creating instances of many small media components is essential (photos, icons, avatars, logos). Let me check component IDs:\n\nPhotos:\n- Photo_Collection: 6:32\n- Photo_Deal_1: 6:39, Photo_Deal_2: 6:43, Photo_Deal_3: 6:44\n- Photo_Hero_Bottom: 6:51, Photo_Hero_Left: 6:72, Photo_Hero_Right: 6:73, Photo_Hero_Top: 6:74\n- Photo_Colorful_Dress: 6:36, Photo_Full_Sweater: 6:50, Photo_Long_Dress: 6:59, Photo_Shiny_Dress: 6:62, Photo_White_Dress: 6:66, Photo_White_Shirt: 6:67\n- Photo_Boxy_Denim_Hat: 6:27, Photo_Dotted_Black_Dress: 6:71, Photo_Linen_Blend_Shirt: 6:57, Photo_Linen_Plain_Top: 6:58, Photo_Long_Sleeve_Coat: 6:60, Photo_Oversized_TShirt: 6:77, Photo_Polarised_Sunglasses: 6:79, Photo_Rockstar_Jacket: 6:61, Photo_Rounded_Red_Hat: 6:78\n- Denim jackets: Black_1 6:45, Black_2 6:70, Blue_1 6:46, Blue_2 6:47, Blue_3 6:48, Lilac 6:49\n- Instagram 1-7: 6:52, 6:53, 6:54, 6:55, 6:56, 6:75, 6:76\n- Photo_Sign_In: 6:80, Photo_Sign_Up: 6:63, Photo_Subscribe_Left: 6:65, Photo_Subscribe_Right: 6:64\n- Avatars: James_K 6:19, Megen_W 6:68, Suzan_B 6:18\n- Logos: Gmail 6:69, Google 6:23\n- Icons: Account 6:15, Cart 6:16, Chevron_Down 6:17, Chevron_Left 6:20, Chevron_Right 6:21, Close 6:22, Compare 6:24, Delivery 6:25, Eye 6:26, Guarantee 6:28, Lock 6:29, Question 6:30, Returns 6:31, Search 6:33, Share 6:34, Shipping 6:35, Star 6:37, Support 6:38, Trophy 6:40, View_Grid 6:41, View_List 6:42\n- Button component: 1:24; Text Field: 1:27\n\nApproach for code: I'll write a helper library in the first call that I re-declare in each subsequent call (since each use_figma call is a fresh execution context — state doesn't persist). So each call must be self-contained. I'll write a compact helpers preamble and include it in every call.\n\nHelpers:\n```js\nconst WS_ID='8:2';\nconst page = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(WS_ID);\nawait figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page);\n// fonts\nconst FONTS=[['Volkhov','Regular'],['Volkhov','Bold'],['Poppins','Regular'],['Poppins','Medium'],['Poppins','Semi Bold'],['Poppins','Bold'],['Poppins','Light']...];\n```\nVolkhov styles: Regular, Bold, Italic, Bold Italic. Poppins: Thin..Black etc. I'll load Regular, Medium, Semi Bold, Bold for Poppins; Regular, Bold for Volkhov.\n\nHelper functions:\n- T(text, {font, size, color, lh, ls, align}) creates text node.\n- R(w,h,fill,r) rectangle.\n- F(name,w,h) frame.\n- inst(id) -> creates instance of component by id. Must handle that component is on another page: `const c = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id); const i = c.createInstance();` — createInstance parents to currentPage? Actually docs: \"The instance is added as a child of the current page\"? Let me recall precisely. In Figma plugin API, `createInstance()` on ComponentNode: \"Creates an instance of this component... The instance will be parented under figma.currentPage.\" I believe it adds to current page. Since I'll set current page to workspace, instances should land there. Then appendChild into target frame. Good.\n\nScaling: instances can be resized. Photos: I want specific crops. Simpler: create a frame with clipContent, put the instance inside, scale instance to cover. For photos, the component likely contains a rectangle with image fill. Resizing the instance stretches the image (image fill scales). Crop by clipping frame. That's acceptable (\"Photos can be cropped freely\").\n\nFor icons, recolor: instance of icon component, then override fills on children: find vector nodes and set fills. I can traverse `i.findAll(n => n.type==='VECTOR'||...)` set fills to ink color. Icons are #1A1A1F neutral; I might keep as is for ink-colored icons, recolor to white where on dark backgrounds, gold for stars, red for urgency eye.\n\nStar ratings: Icon_Star recolored to gold (#F5A623 or similar). Rating color is my choice.\n\nDesign system decisions:\n- Colors:\n  - ink/brand: #000000\n  - text primary: #1A1A1F? Let's use #0A0A0A... Actually simpler: text = #111111 (near-black), secondary = #6B6B6B (gray), background = #FFFFFF, surface = #F7F5F2 (warm off-white) or #FAF9F7. Sale color: #E4572E? The original FASCO uses red-ish sale. Let's pick #D92D20? Hmm, elegant fashion: use #C0392B? I'll choose a refined red: #E0413F? Let me define: sale = #D64541. Rating gold = #F2B234. Success/stock = maybe orange for urgency. Links: black underline. Error: #C1121F.\n- Type scale:\n  - Display (hero ULTIMATE): Volkhov 140\n  - H1: Volkhov 56\n  - H2: Volkhov 42 (section headings)\n  - H3: Volkhov 28\n  - Body: Poppins 16\n  - Small: Poppins 14\n  - Nav: Poppins 16 medium\n- Buttons: black fill, white Poppins medium 16, radius 8? The given Button component — let me inspect what it looks like to reuse consistently. Let me get a screenshot of components page quickly, or get design context. get_design_context isn't in my tool list! The tools available: get_metadata, get_screenshot, use_figma, whoami. The metadata response says \"you MUST call get_design_context\" but that tool isn't available — I have get_metadata and get_screenshot instead. Fine.\n\nLet me screenshot the components frame (1:20) and media icons to see the style. Also maybe screenshot part of the brief? The metadata text gave me all content. A screenshot of the Components frame would show Button and Text Field styling so I can match states.\n\nAlso screenshot the Foundations frame to see exact brand presentation (already text-described). I'll screenshot 1:20 (Components) to see button/field style.\n\nLayout plan (1920 wide, content margin 120? The original FASCO kit used ~ wide). I'll use side padding of 160? Hmm 1920 with 240 padding gives 1440 content. That's clean. Let me use page padding 240? Many fashion sites use narrower content. Let me choose content width 1520 (padding 200)? I'll go with 240 side margins → 1440 content. Actually original FASCO design is 1440 desktop scaled. Here required 1920. I'll use 1440 centered content with 240 margins.\n\nWait, auth pages are 1920×1080 full screen, no header/footer: left media panel + right card, classic.\n\nNow, planning sections per frame:\n\n**01_Home** (1920 × ~7200):\n1. Header (home variant): FASCO wordmark (Volkhov 38?), nav Home/Deals/New Arrivals/Packages, Sign in (text button), Sign Up (black button). Current: Home (bold/underline).\n2. Hero: left Photo_Hero_Left (tall), center Photo_Hero_Top over Photo_Hero_Bottom with ULTIMATE SALE text, right Photo_Hero_Right. Original FASCO hero: left column photo, middle text \"ULTIMATE SALE\" with \"NEW COLLECTION\" and SHOP NOW button, right photo. Brand strip below with wordmarks Minimog Retrolie Brook Learts Vagabond.\n3. Deals Of The Month: heading, copy, countdown boxes (02 Days 06 Hr 05 Mins 30 Sec), Buy Now button, right side slider with 3 deal photos, current slide card \"01 — Spring Sale\" \"30% OFF\", prev/next arrows, indicator 4 slides current 1.\n4. New Arrivals: heading, copy, tabs (Men's Fashion, Women's Fashion current, Women Accessories, Men Accessories, Discount Deals), 6 product cards (photo, FASCO brand, name, stars, (4.1k) Customer Reviews, $95.50, Almost Sold Out), View More button.\n5. Collection banner: Photo_Collection with item labels Flat Cap, Cotton Shirt, Suspender, Leather Shoes, and text Men Collection / Classic Gentleman / DESCRIPTION copy / Size: M / $100.00 / Buy Now.\n6. Testimonials: heading, copy, 3 cards (avatar, quote, 5 stars, name, role), prev/next, current James K.\n7. Instagram: heading, copy, 7 photos row.\n8. Newsletter: Photo_Subscribe_Left | Subscribe To Our Newsletter, copy, email field michael@example.com + Subscribe Now | Photo_Subscribe_Right.\n9. Features strip: 4 features with icons.\n10. Footer (home): FASCO wordmark + links Support Center, Invoicing, Contract, Careers, Blog, FAQs + copyright.\n\n**02_Shop**: header (store variant with icons + nav Home/Shop/Products/Pages, current Shop), page title \"Fashion\" + breadcrumb Home › Fashion, toolbar (Filters left sidebar toggle? Actually \"Filters\" label; sort \"Best selling\" with chevron; view grid/list icons current grid), sidebar filters (Size S M L XL; Colors swatches; Prices list; Brands list with counts? brief just names; Collections list; Tags chips), product grid 9 cards with pagination 1 2 3 next, collection banner, features strip, Instagram, newsletter, footer (store variant: FASCO, Home Shop Products Pages, copyright).\n\n**03_Product**: header current Products, breadcrumb? Not required for product page (not in must-include). Gallery left: main photo Blue_2 + 6 thumbnails. Right: FASCO, Denim Jacket, star rating (3), $39.00 old $59.00, Save 33%; urgency: eye icon \"24 people are viewing this right now\"; \"Hurry up! Sale ends in:\" 00:05:59:47; \"Only 9 item(s) left in stock!\" + stock bar; Size: M with M L XL XXL chips (M selected); Color: Blue with Blue Black Lilac swatches (Blue selected); Quantity stepper − 1 +; Add to cart button; Buy Now? Not required on product (Buy Now is in collection banner and deals). Actions: Compare, Ask a question, Share. Delivery: Estimated Delivery Jul 30 - Aug 03 (Icon_Delivery), Free Shipping & Returns: On all orders over $75 (Icon_Returns), \"Guarantee safe & secure checkout\". People Also Loved section (like deals). Collection banner. Features. Newsletter. Footer store variant.\n\n**04_Mini_Cart**: 03_Product page behind (inactive, dimmed overlay), drawer right: Shopping Cart, close X, free shipping progress \"Buy $36.00 more and get Free Shipping\" + progress bar, item: photo, Denim Jacket, Color : Blue, $39.00, qty stepper 01, gift wrap checkbox unchecked, Subtotal $39.00, Checkout button, View Cart button. Header cart shows count 1.\n\nFor 04, replicating the entire 03 page behind is heavy. I'll build 03 as reusable: make the product page content and then for 04, I could duplicate the frame's children. Simpler approach: build 03 first, then create 04 by cloning the 03 frame (frame.clone() clones without reactions? clone copies nodes; reactions may not copy — actually clone does copy reactions? I believe clone() does not copy prototype interactions in older API... Actually reactions are part of the node and clone() does copy them I think. Let me be careful: I'll add interactions after building 04 via clone, or re-add interactions to both. Simplest: build 03 fully (no reactions), clone to 04 (name 04_Mini_Cart), then dim overlay + drawer on 04, then add reactions to both frames at the end. But wait — cloning a frame with instances is fine.\n\nHmm, but the 04 frame's header cart count needs badge \"1\" while 03 has no count. I can add the badge after cloning by modifying the clone.\n\nAlso 04 needs the behind page \"inactive\" — add a semi-transparent black overlay over the page content within the frame, then drawer on right.\n\n**05_Cart**: header (no current, cart badge 1), title Shopping Cart + breadcrumb Home › Your Shopping Cart, cart table (Product/Price/Quantity/Total columns, row with photo, name, color, Remove, $39.00, stepper, $39.00), cart total box: gift wrap checkbox, Subtotal $39.00, Checkout. Newsletter, footer.\n\n**06_Checkout**: header, title \"FASCO Checkout\", left: Contact (Don't have an account? Create Account, Email Address field), Delivery (Country/Region select, First Name, Last Name, Address, City, Postal Code, Save This Info checkbox), Payment (Credit Card, Card Number w/ lock icon, Expiration Date, Security Code, Card Holder Name, Save This Info checkbox, Pay Now button). Right: order summary (photo w/ qty badge 1, Denim Jacket, Blue, $39.00, Discount code field + Apply, Subtotal $39.00, Shipping $40.00, Total $79.00). Newsletter + footer? Must-include lists Newsletter and Footer for 06, yes. Header with cart count 1, no current nav.\n\n**07_Sign_Up** (1920×1080): left Photo_Sign_Up media panel, right card: FASCO wordmark, \"FASCO Terms & Conditions\", tabs? Form: Create Account heading, First Name, Last Name, Email Address, Phone Number, Password, Confirm Password, Create Account button, OR divider, Sign up with Google, Sign up with Email buttons, \"Already have an account? Login\".\n\nThe original FASCO sign-up has the image on left, form on right with social buttons at top. Layout: FASCO wordmark top, \"FASCO Terms & Conditions\" link top-right.\n\n1080 height with 6 fields + button + social — tight but doable. Field height ~56, gaps 12: title(40) + 6 fields*(56+12)=408 + button 56 + OR + 2 social buttons... total ≈ 800. OK.\n\n**08_Sign_In**: Photo_Sign_In left, form: Sign In To FASCO, Email, Password, Sign In button, Register Now button, Forgot Password? link, OR, social Sign in with Google / Sign in with Email.\n\n**09_Forgot_Password**: Photo_Sign_In, form: Forgot Password, First Name, Last Name, Email Address, Phone Number, Send Confirmation Code, Already have an account? Login.\n\n**10_Confirmation_Code**: Photo_Sign_In, Enter The Confirmation Code, Confirmation Code field, Recover Account, Didn't receive Confirmation Code? Resend Now.\n\n**11_New_Password**: Photo_Sign_In, Enter Your New Password, New Password, Confirm Password, Submit.\n\nPrototype links:\n- 01: SHOP NOW → 02; View More → 02; Sign in → 08; Sign Up → 07.\n- 02: any product card → 03; cart icon → 05; account icon → 08.\n- 03: Add to cart → 04; Shop nav → 02; cart icon → 05.\n- 04: View Cart → 05; Checkout → 06; close → 03.\n- 05: Checkout → 06; Denim Jacket (row) → 03.\n- 06: Create Account → 07; (Pay Now → entry only, no link).\n- 07: Create Account → 01; Login → 08.\n- 08: Sign In → 01; Register Now → 07; Forgot Password? → 09.\n- 09: Send Confirmation Code → 10; Login → 08.\n- 10: Recover Account → 11.\n- 11: Submit → 01.\n\nReactions API: node.reactions = [{trigger:{type:'ON_CLICK'}, actions:[{type:'NODE', destinationId, navigation:'NAVIGATE', transition:{type:'SMART_ANIMATE'...}}]}]... Format: actions: [{type: \"NODE\", destinationId: \"x:y\", navigation: \"NAVIGATE\", transition: {type:'DISSOLVE',duration:{...},easing:{...}}, preserveScrollPosition:false, overlayRelativePosition:null, resetVideoPosition:false, resetScrollPosition:false, resetInteractiveComponents:false}] — simpler: transition null for instant. Use {type:'NODE', destinationId, navigation:'NAVIGATE', transition:null, preserveScrollPosition:false}.\n\nFor the mini cart 04: overlay open would be ideal, but brief says 04 is a frame containing the product page behind with the drawer; links: Add to cart → 04_Mini_Cart (navigate). So simple navigation is fine.\n\nNow, the components requirement: \"Design all other states, the header, the footer, the navigation and every business component as reusable components in your file.\" I should create components in the workspace. Practical approach: build component masters in a \"Components\" area on the workspace page (e.g., a frame \"· Components\" off to the side), then instantiate them into screens. That doubles complexity but is explicitly required (\"Component reuse\" is evaluation criterion #6). \n\nPragmatic plan: create key reusable components:\n- Btn/Primary (black), Btn/Outline? (white with border for View Cart, Register Now), \n- Nav item? maybe skip.\n- Header/Home, Header/Store (with variants via separate components Header/Store/Default, ... too many). Simplify: Header_Home component; Header_Store component (no count, no current) and variants for current page? Components with variants are complex via API. Instead: create a few master components and instances with overrides:\n  - Header/Home (one state — used only on 01)\n  - Header/Store (used on 02–06 with overrides: current nav text styling override + cart badge). Overrides of text color per instance is doable but fiddly to find nodes. Alternative: make Header/Store with no current and no badge; for each page, override. Since finding child nodes by name is easy (i.findOne(n=>n.name===...)), doable.\n- Footer/Home, Footer/Store\n- Card/Product (with photo placeholder? photos differ per instance — swap via overriding the photo instance inside? Nested instances: product card contains an instance of a photo component; to change photo per card, I'd need to replace child. Simpler: product card component contains a rectangle \"photo\" with solid fill; per instance, I traverse to find nested photo instance and swap? There's `swapComponent` for nested instances! If the card master contains an instance of Photo_Shiny_Dress, I can call nestedInst.swapComponent(otherPhotoComp) on the clone... swapComponent is available on instances. That works: after creating card instance, find nested instance, swapComponent(target). But mainComponent must be accessible — need getNodeByIdAsync to get component then swap. OK.\n\nBut honestly, managing overrides for many card variants is complex and error-prone. Evaluation criterion 6 is component reuse — reusing the media components (photos/icons) as instances already counts somewhat, but they clearly want designed components too. \n\nMiddle path: Create these master components on a workspace \"Components\" frame:\n- Button / Primary, Button / Secondary (outline), Button / Ghost\n- Text Field (given default exists; build my own states? brief: default Button and default Text Field are given; \"Design all other states\" — states like focused/filled. I'll create Field/Default usage with the given component, plus a Field/Filled state? To keep scope manageable: reuse given Button + Text Field components for those exact controls where fitting, and create my own components for the rest.\n\nActually, reconsider: the given Button (207×56) and Text Field (420×87) — using them as instances everywhere buttons appear means overriding their labels. Text override in instance: find text node, set characters — fine. But button sizes vary (SHOP NOW big, Buy Now small). Resizing an instance with fixed-layout children may distort. The given Button likely is a frame component with text; resize may not reflow text (no auto-layout?) Unknown. Risky. \n\nDecision: I'll reuse the given components by instancing them in a few canonical places (e.g., the default Button for \"Buy Now\" actions, Text Field in newsletter/checkout/auth forms), resizing as needed and overriding labels. If a resize looks wrong in screenshots, I'll adjust. Actually to be safe and consistent, I'll build my OWN component library (Button component set with my styles) AND place the given Button/Text Field instances somewhere visible? No — the brief says \"Reuse the components in 02 · Components\" per user's task instructions. The file's own brief says they're \"given\" and everything else must be designed. So reuse = use them where default button/field are needed. I'll use them.\n\nLet me check what the given Button and Text Field look like first (screenshot of 1:20) so my design matches. Also check auto-layout presence via a read-only use_figma script querying their structure. That's more reliable: run a script that returns JSON of node 1:24 and 1:27 structure (type, fills, layoutMode, children). Let me do that.\n\nAlso, plan for text: I need to load fonts before creating text. In each call, load fonts at top.\n\nStrategy for build calls (each self-contained with helper preamble):\n\nCall 1: Inspect given Button/Text Field structure (read-only). Also maybe inspect one photo component and icon component structure to know how to recolor/resize.\n\nCall 2: Setup: create workspace structure — tokens, master components frame with core components (buttons, fields, headers, footers, product card, feature item, section heading, tab, pagination, quantity stepper, checkbox, color swatch, size chip, rating stars, newsletter block, instagram strip, collection banner, deals block). This is a lot. Given tool limits (50k chars), I'll split:\n\n- Call 2: helpers + core atoms (Button variants as components, Field, Checkbox, Chip/Size, Swatch, Stars, Stepper, Tab, Pagination, Arrow button, Section heading, Feature item).\n- Call 3: composite components (Header/Home, Header/Store, Footer/Home, Footer/Store, Product card, Collection banner, Deals block, Newsletter, Instagram strip, Testimonial card, Features strip, Filter sidebar, Auth card parts).\n- Call 4: 01_Home assembly.\n- Call 5: 02_Shop assembly.\n- Call 6: 03_Product assembly.\n- Call 7: 04_Mini_Cart (clone 03 + overlay + drawer).\n- Call 8: 05_Cart, 06_Checkout.\n- Call 9: 07–11 auth pages.\n- Call 10: prototype wiring.\n- Calls 11+: screenshots + fixes.\n\nComponent strategy reconsidered for reliability: building masters then overriding text in instances for every usage adds lots of findOne code. Alternative pragmatic approach used by many: make components for things that repeat with identical content (headers need overrides though). Headers repeat with only current-nav/badge differences. I can make Header/Store master WITHOUT current state and badge, then instances get overrides via findOne for the specific page (set text style bold + underline for current, add badge where needed). Badge: master can include badge hidden (visible=false), instances set visible. That's clean!\n\nFooter/Home and Footer/Store: fully static — perfect components.\n\nProduct card: content differs per product. Master contains photo instance + texts; per instance override texts + swap photo. Manageable with a helper that takes config.\n\nTestimonial card: similar with avatar swap + text overrides.\n\nCollection banner, Deals block, Newsletter, Instagram strip, Features strip: identical across pages — perfect components, zero overrides.\n\nSection heading (title+copy): text overrides.\n\nAuth pages: build an Auth/Layout component? Each auth page has different form fields. I'll build them as frames directly but reuse atoms (Field instances, Button instances, social button component, auth card shell component with media instance swapped). Simpler: build directly with shared atoms (Btn, Field components). Good enough reuse.\n\nFor the given Button (1:24) and Text Field (1:27): I'll use instances of them in my flows (e.g., Text Field instances in checkout/auth forms; Button for primary actions), with label overrides. To keep visual consistency, I'll style my other components to match them. Need to inspect their look first.\n\nWait — text override on given Button: if its label is \"Button\" centered, override to \"Buy Now\" etc. Fine.\n\nBut there's a cat